OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-normalized-volume-gap-in-dimension-four-September-24-2026", "title": "The normalized-volume gap in dimension four", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-normalized-volume-gap-in-dimension-four-September-24-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "037", "family_title": "The ordinary-double-point volume gap", "abstract_html": "We prove that every singular complex algebraic klt fourfold germ with zero boundary has normalized volume at most 162, with equality precisely for an analytic ordinary double point. This resolves the ordinary-double-point volume-gap conjecture in dimension four.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\\le162. \\) Equality holds if and only if the analytic germ \\((X,x)\\) is isomorphic to \\( \\left( \\{z_0^2+z_1^2+z_2^2+z_3^2+z_4^2=0\\}\\subset\\mathbb C^5,\\ 0 \\right). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All varieties are over \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(x\\in X\\) be a singular closed point of a normal complex algebraic variety of dimension four. Assume that \\(K_X\\) is \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier and that \\(X\\) is klt near \\(x\\), with boundary zero.</p><p class=\"li\">For a real valuation \\(v\\) centered at \\(x\\), put\n  \\[\n  \\mathfrak a_t(v)=\\{f\\in\\mathcal O_{X,x}:v(f)\\ge t\\},\n  \\qquad\n  \\operatorname{vol}(v)\n  =\n  \\lim_{t\\to\\infty}\n  \\frac{4!\\,\\operatorname{length}(\\mathcal O_{X,x}/\\mathfrak a_t(v))}{t^4}.\n  \\]\n  Its log discrepancy is denoted by \\(A_X(v)\\). For a prime divisor \\(F\\) on a smooth proper birational model \\(\\mu:Y\\to X\\), the convention is\n  \\[\n  A_X(F)=1+\\operatorname{coeff}_F(K_Y-\\mu^*K_X).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The normalized volume is\n  \\[\n  \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\n  =\n  \\inf_{v\\text{ centered at }x}A_X(v)^4\\operatorname{vol}(v),\n  \\]\n  where valuations of infinite discrepancy contribute \\(+\\infty\\). Equivalently,\n  \\[\n  \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\n  =\n  \\inf_{c_X(F)=x}A_X(F)^4\\operatorname{vol}(F);\n  \\]\n  the equivalence follows from the divisorial Kollár-component formulation in [11, Theorem 2.5]. Positive rescaling of a valuation leaves its normalized volume unchanged.</p><p class=\"li\">An ordinary double point means the analytic hypersurface germ defined by a nondegenerate quadratic form in five variables.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\\le162.\n\\]\nEquality holds if and only if the analytic germ \\((X,x)\\) is isomorphic to\n\\[\n\\left(\n\\{z_0^2+z_1^2+z_2^2+z_3^2+z_4^2=0\\}\\subset\\mathbb C^5,\\ 0\n\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>If\n\\[\n\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)&lt;162,\n\\]\nthe bound is already strict. Otherwise, Propositions 1 and 5 give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{edim}(x,X)\\le5.\n\\]</p><p>A singular normal complex algebraic fourfold germ of embedding dimension at most five is a hypersurface. For the algebraic local argument, embed a neighborhood of \\(x\\) in smooth affine space of dimension \\(N\\), and put\n\\[\ne=\\operatorname{edim}(x,X).\n\\]\nThe linear parts of the local defining ideal have rank \\(N-e\\). Choose \\(N-e\\) equations with independent linear parts; their common zero set is smooth of dimension \\(e\\) near \\(x\\) and contains \\(X\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal O_{X,x}\\) is a domain quotient of a regular local ring of dimension \\(e\\). The regular case \\(e=4\\) is excluded by singularity. Hence \\(e=5\\), and the defining ideal is a height-one prime in a regular local ring. Such a ring is a UFD [21, Tag 0AG0], so that prime ideal is principal.</p><p>Liu’s lci theorem [13, Theorem 2.12] now gives\n\\[\n\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\\le162,\n\\]\nand equality forces an ordinary double point.</p><p>Conversely, the ordinary double point has normalized volume\n\\[\n2\\cdot3^4=162\n\\]\nby the same established equality theorem and ODP calculation. Normalized volume is unchanged under isomorphism of completed local rings [13, Lemma 2.15], so this gives the claimed analytic characterization.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "cone reduction", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a positively graded affine fourfold \\(C=\\operatorname{Spec}S\\), with vertex \\(o\\), such that:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(x\\in X\\) satisfy the assumptions of Theorem 1, and suppose\n  \\[\n  V:=\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\\ge162.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a local ring \\((R,\\mathfrak m)\\), write\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{edim}(R)=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\mathfrak m/\\mathfrak m^2.\n  \\]\n  For a positively graded algebra, embedding dimension is taken at its homogeneous maximal ideal.</p><p class=\"li\">For an affine cone with torus character decomposition \\(S=\\bigoplus_\\chi S_\\chi\\) and constant invariant ring, a Reeb vector \\(\\xi\\) satisfies\n  \\[\n  \\langle\\chi,\\xi\\rangle&gt;0\n  \\]\n  for every nonzero occurring character. Its weight valuation is\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{wt}_\\xi\\!\\left(\\sum_\\chi f_\\chi\\right)\n  =\n  \\min_{f_\\chi\\ne0}\\langle\\chi,\\xi\\rangle.\n  \\]\n  An integral cocharacter gives an integer grading, whose weight valuation is called the degree valuation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a positively graded affine fourfold \\(C=\\operatorname{Spec}S\\), with vertex \\(o\\), such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(C\\) is Gorenstein and klt, and \\(C\\setminus\\{o\\}\\) is smooth.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\[\n   \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)=V,\n   \\qquad\n   \\operatorname{edim}(x,X)\\le\\operatorname{edim}(o,C).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. An effective torus acts on \\(C\\), with a minimizing Reeb vector \\(\\xi_*\\). Primitive positive integral gradings along rational rays approaching \\(\\xi_*\\) have degree valuations of discrepancies \\(r\\in\\mathbb Z_{&gt;0}\\) and volumes \\(h&gt;0\\), with\n   \\[\n   r^4h\\ge V,\n   \\qquad\n   r^4h\\longrightarrow V.\n   \\]\n   For each such grading, the canonical module has a homogeneous generator of degree \\(r\\).</p><p>Moreover, \\(o\\) is singular.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply stable degeneration to \\((x,X)\\), with boundary zero, and quotient the acting torus by its kernel. The stable degeneration theorem supplies a quasi-monomial minimizing valuation with finitely generated associated graded ring [24, Theorem 1.2]. The induced Reeb valuation preserves volume and discrepancy and minimizes among all centered valuations [11, Lemma 4.10 and Theorem 1.3]. This gives the volume assertions and, by Lemma 1.1, the embedding-dimension comparison.</p><p>If a singular point of \\(C\\) lies away from \\(o\\), its positive-grading orbit is nonconstant and its closure contains \\(o\\). The singular locus is closed and invariant, so it has dimension at least one at \\(o\\). The nonisolated bound [13, Corollary 2.18] gives\n\\[\nV\\le\\frac{4096}{27}&lt;162,\n\\]\na contradiction.</p><p>Let \\(d\\) be the canonical index at \\(o\\). Its cyclic index-one cover is finite Galois and quasi-étale of degree \\(d\\), and is crepant and klt. It has a single point above \\(o\\). To see this, write its local algebra as the sum of the reflexive canonical powers in residue degrees modulo \\(d\\). A homogeneous element whose power is a unit would itself be a unit; multiplication by it would identify its canonical-power summand with the degree-zero local ring. In nonzero residue degree this would trivialize a proper canonical power, contradicting the definition of \\(d\\). Thus each homogeneous element of nonzero degree is nilpotent in the special fiber, whose reduction is consequently \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p><p>The finite-degree formula [23, Theorem 1.3] and the universal upper bound [13, Theorem 2.11] now give\n\\[\ndV\\le256.\n\\]\nSince \\(V\\ge162\\), necessarily \\(d=1\\).</p><p>The canonical module is locally free at \\(o\\), and its fiber there is one-dimensional. A homogeneous lift of a basis generates the graded module by graded Nakayama. It freely generates because the module has rank one and is torsion-free. Klt singularities are rational and Cohen–Macaulay [7, Theorem 120], so \\(C\\) is Gorenstein.</p><p>Finite generation also proves the required approximation continuity. On finitely many homogeneous generators, nearby Reeb weights lie between \\(1-\\epsilon\\) and \\(1+\\epsilon\\) times the original weights. The same holds on every occurring character, and sandwiches the valuation ideals. Volumes consequently converge. The discrepancy is the canonical-weight linear function [11, Lemma 2.18 and Remark 2.19]. Rational Reeb rays give divisorial valuations up to scale [11, Lemma 2.16]. Dividing an integral cocharacter by its common factor makes it primitive, without changing normalized volume. Since the torus is effective, a primitive cocharacter gives an effective one-parameter action. A homogeneous canonical generator then has an integer degree \\(r\\), equal to the discrepancy of the degree valuation, and \\(r&gt;0\\) by klt. This proves\n\\[\nr^4h\\ge V,\n\\qquad\nr^4h\\longrightarrow V.\n\\]</p><p>Finally, a smooth cone would have \\(\\operatorname{edim}(o,C)=4\\). The embedding-dimension comparison would make \\(x\\) smooth, contrary to hypothesis.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\operatorname{edim}(R)\\le\\operatorname{edim}(\\operatorname{gr}_vR). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(R=\\mathcal O_{X,x}\\) be the local ring of a klt point, with maximal ideal \\(\\mathfrak m_x\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v\\) be a valuation centered at \\(x\\), of finite log discrepancy.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that \\(\\operatorname{gr}_vR\\) is finitely generated.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{edim}(R)\\le\\operatorname{edim}(\\operatorname{gr}_vR).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose minimal homogeneous algebra generators of \\(\\operatorname{gr}_vR\\) and lift them to\n\\[\nf_1,\\ldots,f_e\\in\\mathfrak m_x.\n\\]\nTheir weights are positive. The additive semigroup generated by finitely many positive real numbers has finitely many elements below any fixed bound.</p><p>Given \\(f\\in\\mathfrak m_x\\), subtract a polynomial in the \\(f_i\\) with the same initial form. Unless the remainder is zero, its value strictly increases. Li’s Izumi estimate [8, Theorem 3.1] gives\n\\[\nv(g)\\le M\\operatorname{ord}_{\\mathfrak m_x}(g)\n\\]\nfor a finite constant \\(M\\). Successive cancellation therefore reaches a remainder of value \\(&gt;M\\), or zero, after finitely many steps. That remainder lies in \\(\\mathfrak m_x^2\\). Consequently, the classes of the \\(f_i\\) span \\(\\mathfrak m_x/\\mathfrak m_x^2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 3.4, together with the grading extraction of Paper Lemma 3.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every such integral grading there is a proper birational morphism \\( \\pi:\\widetilde C\\to C, \\) an isomorphism away from \\(o\\), whose reduced exceptional fiber is \\(E\\simeq\\operatorname{Proj}S\\), satisfying …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C=\\operatorname{Spec}S\\) be the singular Gorenstein klt affine fourfold cone obtained in Proposition 1. Thus \\(C\\setminus\\{o\\}\\) is smooth,\n  \\[\n  V=\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)\\ge162,\n  \\]\n  and an effective torus acts with a minimizing Reeb vector \\(\\xi_*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Consider primitive positive integral gradings along rational rays approaching \\(\\xi_*\\). For each such grading, write\n  \\[\n  S=\\bigoplus_{j\\ge0}S_j,\\qquad S_0=\\mathbb C,\n  \\]\n  and let \\(r\\in\\mathbb Z_{&gt;0}\\) be the degree of a homogeneous canonical generator and \\(h\\) the volume of the degree valuation. Then\n  \\[\n  r^4h\\ge V,\\qquad r^4h\\to V.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The action convention is\n  \\[\n  f_j(s\\cdot P)=s^jf_j(P);\n  \\]\n  in particular, \\(df_j\\) has weight \\(j\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every such integral grading there is a proper birational morphism\n\\[\n\\pi:\\widetilde C\\to C,\n\\]\nan isomorphism away from \\(o\\), whose reduced exceptional fiber is \\(E\\simeq\\operatorname{Proj}S\\), satisfying\n\\[\nK_{\\widetilde C}=\\pi^*K_C+(r-1)E.\n\\]\nIts local charts are smooth finite quotient charts in which a homogeneous function of degree \\(j\\) pulls back as \\(u^jf_j|_{\\mathrm{slice}}\\). At a point of \\(E\\) represented by a punctured-cone point with stabilizer order \\(m\\), the completed model is a quotient of smooth parameters \\((u,z_1,z_2,z_3)\\) by \\(\\mu_m\\). The action is faithful on the line coordinate and on the slice tangent space, the quotient is étale in codimension one, \\(mE\\) is locally Cartier, and \\((\\widetilde C,E)\\) is log canonical.</p><p>For all integral gradings sufficiently close to the minimizing ray, every stabilizer order on \\(C\\setminus\\{o\\}\\) satisfies\n\\[\nm&lt;\\frac{r}{5/2}.\n\\]\nConsequently, for every primitive prime divisor \\(F\\) centered at \\(o\\),\n\\[\nA_C(F)\\ge3.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(C\\) is terminal.</p><p>Fixing one grading with this stabilizer bound and rescaling degrees by\n\\[\ny=\\lambda j,\\qquad\n\\lambda=\\frac{5/2}{r},\n\\qquad\nD=\\frac53,\n\\]\nthe canonical weight is \\(5/2\\), and\n\\[\n\\lambda m&lt;1\n\\]\nat every point of \\(C\\setminus\\{o\\}\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\frac32D=\\frac52.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The extraction and its local properties follow from Lemma 2.1.</p><p>Divide the inequality of Lemma 2.2 by \\(r^4h\\) and put\n\\[\n\\delta=\\alpha/r.\n\\]\nIf \\(m/r\\ge2/5\\), then\n\\[\nQ:=mhr^3\n\\ge\\frac25r^4h\n\\ge\\frac{324}{5}&gt;64,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{V}{r^4h}\n\\le\n\\frac{(1+3\\delta)^4}{(1+\\delta Q^{1/3})^3}.\n\\]</p><p>For fixed \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), the right side decreases with \\(Q\\). At \\(Q=324/5\\), its derivative with respect to \\(\\delta\\) at zero is\n\\[\n12-3(324/5)^{1/3}&lt;0.\n\\]\nChoose one sufficiently small positive rational \\(\\delta\\). Its value at \\(Q=324/5\\) is then a fixed number \\(\\kappa&lt;1\\), which bounds the right side for every \\(Q\\ge324/5\\). This contradicts\n\\[\n\\frac{V}{r^4h}\\longrightarrow1\n\\]\nalong any sequence of offending gradings approaching the minimizing ray. Notice that \\(\\alpha=r\\delta\\) is rational. The error constants in Lemma 2.2 need not be uniform in the grading: the cutoff limit was already taken for each individual valuation. Therefore, all sufficiently close gradings satisfy\n\\[\nm&lt;\\frac r{5/2}.\n\\]</p><p>Fix such a grading. Properness centers any primitive prime divisor \\(F\\) centered at \\(o\\) inside \\(E\\). At a point of its center with stabilizer order \\(m\\), local Cartierness gives\n\\[\nm\\operatorname{ord}_F(E)\\in\\mathbb Z_{&gt;0},\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{ord}_F(E)\\ge1/m.\n\\]\nUsing the lc pair from Lemma 2.1,\n\\[\nA_C(F)\n=\nA_{\\widetilde C,E}(F)+r\\operatorname{ord}_F(E)\n\\ge r/m&gt;5/2.\n\\]\nSince \\(K_C\\) is Cartier, \\(A_C(F)\\) is an integer, proving\n\\[\nA_C(F)\\ge3.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(C\\) is terminal, since it is smooth away from \\(o\\).</p><p>Finally, rescale degrees by\n\\[\ny=\\lambda j,\\qquad\n\\lambda=\\frac{5/2}{r},\\qquad\nD=\\frac53.\n\\]\nThe canonical weight becomes \\(5/2\\), and the stabilizer inequality becomes\n\\[\n\\lambda m&lt;1.\n\\]\nThe primitive orders are unchanged by this rescaling. The identity\n\\[\n\\frac32D=\\frac52\n\\]\nshows that a boundary constructed from a threshold at most \\(3/2\\) and functions of weights below \\(D\\) has total weight strictly below the canonical weight.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a proper birational morphism \\( \\pi:\\widetilde C\\to C, \\) an isomorphism away from \\(o\\), with reduced exceptional fiber \\(E\\), such that \\( K_{\\widetilde C}=\\pi^*K_C+(r-1)E. \\) At a point of \\(E\\) represented …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C=\\operatorname{Spec}S\\) be a normal Gorenstein affine fourfold cone, smooth away from its vertex \\(o\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an effective positive integral grading\n  \\[\n  S=\\bigoplus_{j\\ge0}S_j,\\qquad S_0=\\mathbb C.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let a homogeneous generator of the canonical module have degree \\(r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the convention \\(f_j(s\\cdot P)=s^jf_j(P)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a proper birational morphism\n\\[\n\\pi:\\widetilde C\\to C,\n\\]\nan isomorphism away from \\(o\\), with reduced exceptional fiber \\(E\\), such that\n\\[\nK_{\\widetilde C}=\\pi^*K_C+(r-1)E.\n\\]\nAt a point of \\(E\\) represented by a punctured-cone point with stabilizer order \\(m\\), the completed local model is a smooth quotient with parameters \\((u,z_1,z_2,z_3)\\). The group \\(\\mu_m\\) acts faithfully on the line coordinate and faithfully on the slice tangent space. The quotient is étale in codimension one, \\(mE\\) is locally Cartier, and \\((\\widetilde C,E)\\) is log canonical.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the coarse quotient of\n\\[\n(C\\setminus\\{o\\})\\times\\mathbb A^1_u\n\\]\nby the grading action on the first factor and weight \\(-1\\) on \\(u\\). The map to \\(C\\) is defined by\n\\[\nf_j\\longmapsto u^jf_j(P),\n\\]\nand \\(E\\simeq\\operatorname{Proj}S\\) is the reduced zero section.</p><p>For a homogeneous \\(g\\in S_d\\) nonzero at a base point, use the slice \\(g=1\\). It is smooth: the orbit derivative of \\(g\\) on this level set is the nonzero number \\(d\\). The chart is the quotient of the slice times \\(\\mathbb A^1\\) by \\(\\mu_d\\), and the corresponding chart of \\(\\operatorname{Proj}S\\) is the quotient of the slice.</p><p>If \\(B\\) is the slice ring, then \\(B\\) is finite over \\(B^{\\mu_d}\\), while \\(u^d\\) is the pullback of \\(g\\). Thus \\(B[u]\\), and then its invariant ring, is finite over the image of the ring of the corresponding chart of \\(C\\times\\operatorname{Proj}S\\). The morphism into \\(C\\times\\operatorname{Proj}S\\) is finite, so \\(\\pi\\) is proper. For \\(u\\ne0\\) it is visibly an isomorphism.</p><p>At a point of the zero section, the completed local quotient is the quotient by its stabilizer \\(\\mu_m\\). The stabilizer action on \\(u\\) is faithful. If an element acted trivially on the slice tangent space, it would also act trivially on the orbit tangent line, hence on the tangent space of the smooth punctured cone. Finite-group linearization would make it the identity near the point, and then on the irreducible cone. Effectiveness excludes this for a nonidentity element. Hence the slice representation is faithful.</p><p>A nonidentity element fixes only \\(u=0\\) and a proper subset of the slice, so the total quotient has no codimension-one ramification. In the completed quotient, the invariant equation \\(u^m\\) cuts out \\(mE\\). Cartierness descends from completion, which is faithfully flat, so \\(mE\\) is locally Cartier on \\(\\widetilde C\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\eta\\) be the homogeneous canonical generator. On the smooth chart its pullback has the form\n\\[\nu^{r-1}\\,du\\wedge\\beta,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\beta\\) is a nonvanishing local top form on the slice. This follows from homogeneity and from the absence of zeros or poles away from \\(u=0\\). The quotient is étale in codimension one, giving the asserted relative canonical coefficient. Finally, the smooth pair with boundary \\(u=0\\) is lc and descends to \\((\\widetilde C,E)\\). This is also the orbifold-cone construction in [12, Section 2 and Proposition 2.1].</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every rational \\(\\alpha>0\\), \\( V\\le (r+3\\alpha)^4 \\frac{h}{\\bigl(1+\\alpha(mh)^{1/3}\\bigr)^3}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work with the cone and one effective positive integral grading in Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(r\\) be its canonical degree and \\(h\\) the volume of its degree valuation. Equivalently,\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{j\\ge0}(\\dim S_j)e^{-sj}\n  =\n  \\frac{h}{s^4}+O(s^{-3})\n  \\qquad(s\\downarrow0).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V=\\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose a point of \\(C\\setminus\\{o\\}\\) has stabilizer order \\(m\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every rational \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nV\\le\n(r+3\\alpha)^4\n\\frac{h}{\\bigl(1+\\alpha(mh)^{1/3}\\bigr)^3}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>On the smooth algebraic slice cover of a chart in Lemma 2.1, take the monomial valuation \\(w_\\alpha\\) with weights\n\\[\n(1,\\alpha,\\alpha,\\alpha)\n\\]\nin \\(u\\) and regular slice parameters centered at the chosen lift. Restrict it to the subfield \\(K(C)\\), setting\n\\[\nv_\\alpha=w_\\alpha|_{K(C)}.\n\\]\nThis valuation is proportional to a prime divisor over \\(C\\); retain this scaling instead of normalizing its value group. It is centered exactly at \\(o\\), since every positive-degree function has positive value. Rational monomial valuations may be placed on smooth proper birational models by resolution, as in [6, Theorems 26–27].</p><p>The finite-map discrepancy formula on the algebraic slice cover, which is étale in codimension one, and Lemma 2.1 give\n\\[\nA_C(v_\\alpha)=(1+3\\alpha)+(r-1)=r+3\\alpha.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(f_j\\in S_j\\),\n\\[\nv_\\alpha(f_j)\n=\nj+\\alpha\\operatorname{ord}(f_j|_{\\mathrm{slice}}).\n\\]\nA sum of distinct homogeneous degrees takes the minimum of these values: the corresponding Taylor series have distinct powers of the independent variable \\(u\\).</p><p>For a cutoff \\(N\\), the image of \\(S_j\\), \\(0\\le j&lt;N\\), in the quotient by the valuation ideal is the image of its slice-jet evaluation map. Its dimension is at most\n\\[\n\\min\\left\\{\n\\dim S_j,\\\n\\dim\\left(\n\\text{slice jets of order }&lt;(N-j)/\\alpha\n\\text{ in character }j\n\\right)\n\\right\\}.\n\\]</p><p>After linearizing the cyclic slice action, its three character weights generate \\(\\mathbb Z/m\\mathbb Z\\). Counting nonnegative lattice points in a simplex in a fixed congruence class therefore gives\n\\[\n\\dim(\\text{one character of jets of order }&lt;\\ell)\n=\n\\frac{\\ell^3}{6m}+O((\\ell+1)^2).\n\\]\nFor example, in boxes of side \\(m\\), each character has exactly \\(m^2\\) representatives; boxes meeting the simplex boundary contribute \\(O(\\ell^2)\\).</p><p>Also,\n\\[\n\\dim S_j=\\frac h6j^3+O((j+1)^2).\n\\]\nTo see that its leading coefficient has no periodic oscillation, choose a period for the eventual Hilbert quasipolynomial. Multiplication by a nonzero homogeneous element injects one residue class into its translate, comparing their cubic leading coefficients. Iterating the translation around its finite cycle forces equality. Since the grading is effective, the occurring degrees generate \\(\\mathbb Z\\); all residue classes thus have the same leading coefficient, whose value is determined by the expansion defining \\(h\\).</p><p>Summing the two estimates, multiplying by \\(4!/N^4\\), and taking \\(N\\to\\infty\\) gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}(v_\\alpha)\n\\le\n4\\int_0^1\n\\min\\left\\{\nhz^3,\\frac{(1-z)^3}{m\\alpha^3}\n\\right\\}\\,dz.\n\\]\nLocalization at the vertex does not change these finite lengths. The two integrands cross at\n\\[\nz_0=\\bigl(1+\\alpha(mh)^{1/3}\\bigr)^{-1};\n\\]\nintegration gives\n\\[\n4\\int_0^1\n\\min\\left\\{\nhz^3,\\frac{(1-z)^3}{m\\alpha^3}\n\\right\\}\\,dz\n=\n\\frac{h}{\\bigl(1+\\alpha(mh)^{1/3}\\bigr)^3}.\n\\]\nTesting the divisorial infimum with \\(v_\\alpha\\) proves the asserted inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "short-interval nonvanishing", "depth": 0, "snippet": "(a) \\(S\\) has a nonzero homogeneous element of weight in \\((0,D)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put \\(D=5/3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For part (a), let \\(S\\) be the coordinate ring of the cone in Proposition 1, equipped with a grading fixed as in Proposition 2. Integer degrees are rescaled by\n  \\[\n  y=\\lambda j,\\qquad \\lambda=\\frac{5/2}{r}.\n  \\]\n  Thus the canonical generator has weight \\(5/2\\), every nonvertex stabilizer order \\(m\\) satisfies \\(\\lambda m&lt;1\\), and the degree valuation has volume \\(h\\) with \\(r^4h\\ge162\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For part (b), let \\(T\\) be a finitely generated positively graded normal Cohen–Macaulay domain with \\(T_0=\\mathbb C\\), of dimension \\(n=2\\) or \\(3\\). Integer degrees are rescaled by a positive number \\(\\lambda\\), so weights lie in \\(\\lambda\\mathbb Z\\). Assume every nonvertex stabilizer order \\(m\\) satisfies \\(\\lambda m&lt;1\\); the action on \\(T\\) need not be effective.</p><p class=\"li\">The canonical module \\(\\omega_T\\) carries the natural action on forms. For a rank-one reflexive module \\(M\\), write\n  \\[\n  M^{[q]}=(M^{\\otimes q})^{**}.\n  \\]\n  Assume in part (b) that, for some integer \\(q\\ge1\\), there is a homogeneous injection\n  \\[\n  \\omega_T^{[q]}\\longrightarrow T\n  \\]\n  lowering weights by a positive amount.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>(a) \\(S\\) has a nonzero homogeneous element of weight in \\((0,D)\\).</p><p>(b) \\(T\\) has a nonzero homogeneous element of weight in \\((0,D)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For the four-dimensional ring \\(S\\), put\n\\[\np=\\frac54\n\\]\nand translate the coordinate by \\(x=y+p\\). The canonical generator has weight \\(2p\\), so the measure of Lemma 3.1 becomes odd: a positive atom at \\(x=y+p\\ge p\\) is paired with its negative mirror. The constant function gives masses \\(1,-1\\) at \\(p,-p\\).</p><p>The polynomial density is therefore odd as well, by uniqueness of the zero-frequency part in Lemma 3.1. Indeed, arbitrary Fourier-transform jets at zero detect every coefficient of a polynomial. Write it as\n\\[\na(x^3+cx),\n\\qquad\na=\\frac{h}{6\\lambda^4}\n=\n\\frac{r^4h}{6(5/2)^4}\n\\ge\\frac{432}{625}.\n\\]</p><p>The test \\(x|g_2(x)|^2\\), understood through the approximants in Lemma 3.3, has nonnegative pairing with the odd measure. The second and fourth moments both equal one third of the mass, so this test gives\n\\[\n\\frac{a(1+c)}3\\int_{\\mathbb R}|g_2|^2\\ge0.\n\\]\nHence \\(c\\ge-1\\).</p><p>Suppose that no function has weight in \\((0,D)\\), and put\n\\[\nl=p+D=\\frac{35}{12}.\n\\]\nFor an even transform \\(g\\), the test\n\\[\nx(l^2-x^2)|g(x)|^2\n\\]\nhas nonpositive signed contributions at every supported atom except the pair at \\(\\pm p\\). At \\(\\pm l\\) it vanishes. Hence\n\\[\na\\int_{\\mathbb R}(x^4+cx^2)(l^2-x^2)|g(x)|^2\\,dx\n\\le\n2p(l^2-p^2)|g(p)|^2.\n\\]</p><p>Write\n\\[\nM_{2j}=\\int_{\\mathbb R}x^{2j}|g(x)|^2\\,dx.\n\\]\nThe left side is\n\\[\na\\bigl[(l^2M_4-M_6)+c(l^2M_2-M_4)\\bigr].\n\\]\nWe seek \\(l^2M_2-M_4&gt;0\\), so that \\(c\\ge-1\\) gives a lower bound. The remaining moment expression must dominate the contribution of the constant atoms.</p><p>Choose\n\\[\ng(x)=g_3(x-1)+g_3(x+1).\n\\]\nIts frequency profile is\n\\[\n2\\cos(2\\pi t)\\cos^3(\\pi t)\n=\n\\cos(\\pi t)+\\frac34\\cos(3\\pi t)+\\frac14\\cos(5\\pi t).\n\\]\nIt has even smooth approximants in \\(H^3\\). Orthogonality of these three modes and Plancherel give\n\\[\nM_{2j}\n=\n\\frac12\\left[\n(1/2)^{2j}\n+\\frac9{16}(3/2)^{2j}\n+\\frac1{16}(5/2)^{2j}\n\\right],\n\\qquad j=0,1,2,3.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nM_2=\\frac{61}{64},\n\\qquad\nM_4=\\frac{685}{256},\n\\qquad\nM_6=\\frac{11101}{1024}.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\nl^2M_2-M_4=\\frac{50065}{9216}&gt;0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nl^2(M_4-M_2)-M_6+M_4=\\frac{26581}{4096}&gt;0.\n\\]\nUsing first \\(c\\ge-1\\) and then \\(a\\ge432/625\\), the left side of the support inequality is at least\n\\[\n\\frac{432}{625}\\cdot\\frac{26581}{4096}\n=\n\\frac{717687}{160000}.\n\\]</p><p>Direct integration gives\n\\[\ng_3(v)\n=\n\\frac{12\\cos(\\pi v)}\n{\\pi(1-4v^2)(9-4v^2)},\n\\]\nwith removable singularities understood. Therefore\n\\[\ng(p)\n=\n\\frac{1088\\sqrt2}{1155\\pi}\n&lt;\n\\frac{544}{1155}\n&lt;\n\\frac12.\n\\]\nThe right side of the support inequality is consequently less than \\(625/144\\). This is impossible, since\n\\[\n\\frac{717687}{160000}-\\frac{625}{144}\n=\n\\frac{209183}{1440000}&gt;0.\n\\]\nEvery integral used here has polynomial degree at most six after multiplication by the density, so the \\(H^3\\) approximation in Lemma 3.3 suffices. This proves part (a).</p><p>For part (b) in dimension three, Lemma 3.2 gives\n\\[\nP_T(y)=a(y^2+uy+c),\n\\qquad a&gt;0,\\quad u&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe measure \\(\\mu_T\\) is positive. Testing with \\(|g_1(y+u/2)|^2\\) and using Lemma 3.3 gives\n\\[\nc-u^2/4\\ge-1/4.\n\\]</p><p>All reflected canonical atoms lie at strictly negative weights, by Lemma 3.2. If \\(T\\) has no weights in \\((0,D)\\), the measure has no atom in that interval. Put\n\\[\nd=D/2=\\frac56.\n\\]\nThe test\n\\[\ny(D-y)|g_2(y-d)|^2\n\\]\nis nonpositive on the support of the measure, so its integral is at most zero. After division by \\(a\\) and by the mass of \\(|g_2|^2\\), its polynomial integral is\n\\[\n\\left(d^2-\\frac13\\right)(d^2+ud+c)\n+\\frac{d^2}{3}-\\frac13.\n\\]\nBecause \\(d^2-1/3&gt;0\\), \\(u&gt;0\\), and \\(c\\ge u^2/4-1/4\\), this is at least\n\\[\n\\left(d^2-\\frac13\\right)\\left(d^2-\\frac14\\right)\n+\\frac{d^2}{3}-\\frac13\n=\n\\frac{19}{324}&gt;0,\n\\]\na contradiction.</p><p>In dimension two,\n\\[\nP_T(y)=a(y+c),\n\\qquad a,c&gt;0,\n\\]\nand the reflected canonical atoms are negative both in location and in sign. If \\((0,D)\\) contains no function weight, the test\n\\[\ny^2(D-y)|g_2(y-\\beta)|^2,\n\\qquad\n\\beta=\\frac23,\n\\]\nhas nonpositive pairing with \\(\\mu_T\\): it is nonnegative at negative locations, vanishes at zero, and is nonpositive at every positive atom.</p><p>Using the centered moments in Lemma 3.3, its polynomial integral divided by \\(a\\) and the mass is\n\\[\n(D-\\beta)\\beta^3\n+\\frac{3D\\beta-6\\beta^2}{3}\n-\\frac13\n+c\\left((D-\\beta)\\beta^2+\\frac{D-3\\beta}{3}\\right)\n=\n\\frac5{27}+\\frac c3&gt;0.\n\\]\nThis is the final contradiction. The last two cases use moments through degree four, supplied by the \\(H^2\\) approximation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Hilbert-series identity", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(F\\) is a Schwartz function whose smooth Fourier transform is supported in \\((-1,1)\\), then \\( \\int_{\\mathbb R}F\\,d\\mu_T = \\int_{\\mathbb R}F(y)P_T(y)\\,dy. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\) be a finitely generated positively graded normal Cohen–Macaulay domain with \\(T_0=\\mathbb C\\) and dimension \\(n\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Integer degrees are rescaled by \\(\\lambda&gt;0\\), so all weights lie in \\(\\lambda\\mathbb Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The canonical module \\(\\omega_T\\) carries the natural action on forms.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume every nonvertex stabilizer order \\(m\\) satisfies \\(\\lambda m&lt;1\\). The action need not be effective.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  H_T(s)=\\sum_y(\\dim T_y)e^{-sy}.\n  \\]\n  If its polar part at zero is\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{a_jj!}{s^{j+1}},\n  \\]\n  define\n  \\[\n  P_T(y)=\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}a_jy^j.\n  \\]\n  Its leading coefficient is positive.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the signed measure, whose coefficients have polynomial growth,\n  \\[\n  \\mu_T\n  =\n  \\sum_y\\dim T_y\\,\\delta_y\n  +\n  (-1)^{n-1}\\sum_y\\dim(\\omega_T)_y\\,\\delta_{-y}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the Fourier-transform convention\n  \\[\n  \\widehat F(t)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}F(y)e^{-2\\pi iyt}\\,dy.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(F\\) is a Schwartz function whose smooth Fourier transform is supported in \\((-1,1)\\), then\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}F\\,d\\mu_T\n=\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}F(y)P_T(y)\\,dy.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write the integer-graded Hilbert series as \\(H(z)\\), where \\(z=e^{-\\lambda s}\\). It is rational with denominator a product of factors \\(1-z^d\\).</p><p>A pole at a root of unity \\(\\zeta\\ne1\\) requires a point other than the vertex fixed by the grading element \\(\\zeta\\). Indeed, if no such point exists, the homogeneous functions whose characters at \\(\\zeta\\) are nontrivial cut out only the vertex. Choose finitely many of them with the same zero locus. Their Koszul homology modules have finite length, and the graded Euler characteristic gives\n\\[\nH(z)\\prod_i(1-z^{d_i})=\\text{a polynomial}.\n\\]\nNone of the factors on the left vanishes at \\(\\zeta\\), excluding a pole there.</p><p>If such a pole root has order \\(d\\), then \\(d\\) divides the stabilizer order of a nonvertex fixed point. Thus \\(\\lambda d&lt;1\\). Every nonzero frequency \\(t\\) with\n\\[\ne^{-2\\pi i\\lambda t}=\\zeta\n\\]\nhas absolute value at least \\(1/(\\lambda d)&gt;1\\). For the identity root, the nonzero frequencies are \\(k/\\lambda\\), also outside \\([-1,1]\\), because the stabilizer assumption implies \\(\\lambda&lt;1\\).</p><p>Graded canonical duality gives\n\\[\nH_{\\omega_T}(z)=(-1)^nH(z^{-1}).\n\\]\nOne can obtain this identity by dualizing a graded free resolution over a weighted polynomial ring into its canonical module; Cohen–Macaulayness leaves just the relevant Ext module. This fixes the grading by the natural action on forms. See also [20, Theorem 6.1(ii)].</p><p>It follows that \\(\\mu_T\\) is the coefficient measure of the expansion of \\(H\\) at zero minus its expansion at infinity. A finite Laurent-polynomial part cancels exactly between these expansions. For a partial-fraction term\n\\[\n(1-z/\\zeta)^{-\\ell},\n\\]\nthe difference has, at every integer \\(k\\), coefficient\n\\[\n\\binom{k+\\ell-1}{\\ell-1}\\zeta^{-k},\n\\]\nwhere the binomial is interpreted as a polynomial in \\(k\\). Thus \\(\\mu_T\\) is a sum of polynomially weighted lattice combs at the pole frequencies.</p><p>Poisson summation for polynomial times Schwartz functions now annihilates every nonzero frequency. If \\(Q(k)\\) is the coefficient polynomial at the root \\(1\\), the surviving density is\n\\[\nQ(y/\\lambda)/\\lambda.\n\\]\nThis is exactly \\(P_T(y)\\): the polar part obtained by summing each monomial on the positive lattice is its Laplace integral, using\n\\[\n\\int_0^\\infty y^je^{-sy}\\,dy=\\frac{j!}{s^{j+1}}.\n\\]\nOther roots give functions holomorphic at \\(s=0\\). This proves the identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "canonical-module positivity", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every nonzero homogeneous section of \\(\\omega_T\\) has positive weight, and the coefficient of \\(y^{n-2}\\) in \\(P_T(y)\\) is positive.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\) be a finitely generated positively graded normal Cohen–Macaulay domain with \\(T_0=\\mathbb C\\), of dimension \\(n\\ge2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use rescaled weights and the natural grading on \\(\\omega_T\\), and define \\(P_T\\) from the polar part of \\(H_T\\) as in Lemma 3.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that, for some integer \\(q\\ge1\\), there is a homogeneous injection\n  \\[\n  \\omega_T^{[q]}\\longrightarrow T\n  \\]\n  lowering weights by \\(e&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every nonzero homogeneous section of \\(\\omega_T\\) has positive weight, and the coefficient of \\(y^{n-2}\\) in \\(P_T(y)\\) is positive.</p>", "proof": "<p>A canonical section of weight \\(w\\) has nonzero \\(q\\)-th power in the rank-one reflexive module. Its image under the injection has weight\n\\[\nqw-e\\ge0,\n\\]\nso \\(w\\ge e/q&gt;0\\).</p><p>Write the two highest pole terms as\n\\[\nH_T(s)=\\frac L{s^n}+\\frac{L_1}{s^{n-1}}+\\cdots.\n\\]\nBy graded canonical duality, those for \\(\\omega_T\\) are\n\\[\n\\frac L{s^n}-\\frac{L_1}{s^{n-1}}.\n\\]</p><p>For a rank-one graded reflexive module \\(M\\), denote its subleading coefficient by \\(B(M)\\). We claim\n\\[\nB(M^{[q]})-B(T)\n=\nq\\bigl(B(M)-B(T)\\bigr).\n\\]\nChoose a nonzero homogeneous section of \\(M\\), of weight \\(a\\), and form\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow T(-a)\\longrightarrow M\\longrightarrow Q\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nEvery rank-one module here has leading coefficient \\(L\\), and\n\\[\nB(M)-B(T)=-aL+b(Q),\n\\]\nwhere \\(b(Q)\\) is the coefficient of \\(s^{-(n-1)}\\) in \\(H_Q\\).</p><p>A graded prime filtration expresses \\(b(Q)\\) as the sum, over height-one primes \\(\\mathfrak p\\), of the length of \\(Q_{\\mathfrak p}\\) times the leading coefficient of \\(T/\\mathfrak p\\). Shifts of these factors do not change that leading coefficient. At such a prime, \\(T_{\\mathfrak p}\\) is a DVR and \\(M_{\\mathfrak p}\\) is free of rank one. Powering the chosen section multiplies its zero order, hence that quotient length, by \\(q\\); its weight becomes \\(qa\\). This proves the claim. Terms in codimension at least two affect only lower poles, so reflexive powers need not be Cohen–Macaulay.</p><p>Applying the claim to \\(\\omega_T\\) gives\n\\[\nB(\\omega_T^{[q]})=(1-2q)L_1.\n\\]\nThe image of the assumed injection has Hilbert series\n\\[\ne^{es}H_{\\omega_T^{[q]}}(s).\n\\]\nThe quotient in \\(T\\) has dimension at most \\(n-1\\), so its coefficient of \\(s^{-(n-1)}\\) is nonnegative. Hence\n\\[\n(1-2q)L_1+eL\\le L_1.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nL_1\\ge\\frac{eL}{2q}&gt;0.\n\\]\nSince \\(L_1=(n-2)!a_{n-2}\\), the claimed sign follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The zero-extended frequency profile \\(\\cos^b(\\pi t)\\mathbf 1_{[-1/2,1/2]}(t)\\) belongs to \\(H^b(\\mathbb R)\\), and smooth functions supported strictly inside \\((-1/2,1/2)\\) approximate it in \\(H^b\\).", "statement": "<p>**Fourier profiles and approximation, from the preparation for Paper Proposition 4.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For \\(b=1,2,3\\), define\n  \\[\n  g_b(x)=\\int_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\cos^b(\\pi t)e^{2\\pi ixt}\\,dt.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H^b(\\mathbb R)\\) denote the Sobolev space of square-integrable functions whose weak derivatives through order \\(b\\) are square-integrable.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The zero-extended frequency profile \\(\\cos^b(\\pi t)\\mathbf 1_{[-1/2,1/2]}(t)\\) belongs to \\(H^b(\\mathbb R)\\), and smooth functions supported strictly inside \\((-1/2,1/2)\\) approximate it in \\(H^b\\). Their transforms converge in weighted \\(L^2\\), controlling all moments of the squared transform through degree \\(2b\\), and at every fixed point.</p><p>For each smooth approximant, a polynomial times its squared absolute transform is a valid test in Lemma 3.1. The same approximation properties hold under translation and the finite linear combinations used in the proof of Proposition 3; parity can be preserved.</p><p>The centered moments satisfy\n\\[\n\\frac{\\int_{\\mathbb R}x^2|g_1(x)|^2\\,dx}\n{\\int_{\\mathbb R}|g_1(x)|^2\\,dx}\n=\\frac14,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{\\int_{\\mathbb R}x^2|g_2(x)|^2\\,dx}\n{\\int_{\\mathbb R}|g_2(x)|^2\\,dx}\n=\n\\frac{\\int_{\\mathbb R}x^4|g_2(x)|^2\\,dx}\n{\\int_{\\mathbb R}|g_2(x)|^2\\,dx}\n=\\frac13.\n\\]\nThe masses are\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}|g_1(x)|^2\\,dx=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}|g_2(x)|^2\\,dx=\\frac38.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The boundary traces of the zero-extended frequency profile through order \\(b-1\\) vanish. Hence the profile belongs to \\(H^b(\\mathbb R)\\), and smooth functions supported strictly inside \\((-1/2,1/2)\\) approximate it in \\(H^b\\). Their transforms converge in weighted \\(L^2\\), controlling all moments of the squared transform through degree \\(2b\\), and at every fixed point by \\(L^1\\) convergence of the profiles. Translation and finite linear combinations preserve these properties; parity can be preserved.</p><p>For each smooth approximant, the Fourier transform of its squared absolute transform is a convolution supported compactly inside \\((-1,1)\\), and polynomial multiplication differentiates it without enlarging its support. Thus a polynomial times the squared absolute transform is a valid test in Lemma 3.1.</p><p>For the applications, first obtain a support inequality for each approximating test and then pass only its polynomial integrals and finitely many fixed evaluations to the limit. No interchange of a limit and an infinite discrete sum is needed.</p><p>The moment identities and masses follow from Plancherel by integrating squared derivatives of the profiles, with factors \\((2\\pi)^{-2j}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "the maximal-ideal log canonical threshold", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\operatorname{lct}_o(C;\\mathfrak m_o)>\\frac32. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C=\\operatorname{Spec}S\\) be the cone of Proposition 1, with vertex \\(o\\), and fix a grading as in Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  y=\\lambda j,\\qquad\n  \\lambda=\\frac{5/2}{r},\\qquad\n  D=\\frac53.\n  \\]\n  The canonical weight is \\(5/2\\), and every nonvertex stabilizer order \\(m\\) satisfies \\(\\lambda m&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathfrak m_o\\) be the maximal ideal of \\(o\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a pair \\((W,\\Delta)\\) with \\(K_W+\\Delta\\) \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier,\n  \\[\n  A_{W,\\Delta}(F)\n  =\n  1+\\operatorname{coeff}_F\\bigl(K_Y-\\mu^*(K_W+\\Delta)\\bigr).\n  \\]\n  The pair is klt, respectively lc, when all prime divisors over it have positive, respectively nonnegative, log discrepancies. An lc center is the center of a divisor of log discrepancy zero; a minimal lc center is minimal under inclusion.</p><p class=\"li\">For an ideal \\(\\mathfrak a\\), \\(\\operatorname{lct}_o(W;\\mathfrak a)\\) means the threshold for log canonicity in a neighborhood of \\(o\\). Its valuative formula allows centers containing \\(o\\). For \\(\\mathfrak a=\\mathfrak m_o\\), only divisors centered exactly at \\(o\\) have positive order on the ideal.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{lct}_o(C;\\mathfrak m_o)&gt;\\frac32.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose\n\\[\n\\operatorname{lct}_o(C;\\mathfrak m_o)\\le\\frac32.\n\\]\nLemma 4.1 constructs a homogeneous lc boundary \\(G\\) of total weight less than \\(5/2\\), on whose lc centers every function of weight in \\((0,D)\\) vanishes. Lemma 4.2 excludes the vertex as an lc center and supplies a proper minimal lc center \\(Z\\) with\n\\[\n1\\le\\dim Z\\le3.\n\\]\nIts coordinate ring \\(T\\) is normal and Cohen–Macaulay, satisfies the stabilizer hypothesis of Proposition 3(b), and has\n\\[\nT_y=0\\qquad(0&lt;y&lt;D).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(\\dim Z=1\\), Lemma 4.3 contradicts this vanishing.</p><p>If \\(\\dim Z=2\\), Lemma 4.4 supplies the homogeneous injection from a reflexive canonical power into \\(T\\), lowering weights by a positive amount. Proposition 3(b) then contradicts the vanishing.</p><p>If \\(\\dim Z=3\\), Lemma 4.5 supplies the same required injection, and Proposition 3(b) again contradicts the vanishing.</p><p>All possibilities are excluded. Therefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{lct}_o(C;\\mathfrak m_o)&gt;\\frac32.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Set \\( t=\\operatorname{lct}_o(C;\\mathfrak b^{d_0/B}). \\) Then \\( 0<t\\le\\frac32, \\qquad td_0<\\frac52. \\) There is an effective homogeneous rational boundary \\(G\\), of total boundary weight \\(td_0\\), such that \\((C,G)\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Homogeneous boundary construction, from the proof of Paper Proposition 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in the setting of Proposition 4 and assume\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{lct}_o(C;\\mathfrak m_o)\\le\\frac32.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Take a homogeneous vector-space basis \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_s\\) of\n  \\[\n  \\bigoplus_{0&lt;y&lt;D}S_y.\n  \\]\n  This space is finite-dimensional and nonzero by Proposition 3(a).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  w_i=\\operatorname{wt}(f_i),\\qquad\n  d_0=\\max_iw_i&lt;D.\n  \\]\n  Choose a common weight \\(B\\) such that every \\(B/w_i\\) is a positive integer, and put\n  \\[\n  \\mathfrak b=(f_i^{B/w_i}:1\\le i\\le s).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Set\n\\[\nt=\\operatorname{lct}_o(C;\\mathfrak b^{d_0/B}).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n0&lt;t\\le\\frac32,\n\\qquad\ntd_0&lt;\\frac52.\n\\]\nThere is an effective homogeneous rational boundary \\(G\\), of total boundary weight \\(td_0\\), such that \\((C,G)\\) is globally lc, has an lc center, and every lc center lies in the common zero locus of the \\(f_i\\). Every lc center is invariant under the grading action and contains \\(o\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For every divisor \\(F\\) centered at \\(o\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{d_0}{B}\\operatorname{ord}_F(\\mathfrak b)\n=\n\\min_i\\frac{d_0}{w_i}\\operatorname{ord}_F(f_i)\n\\ge\n\\operatorname{ord}_F(\\mathfrak m_o).\n\\]\nThese divisors suffice to test the maximal-ideal threshold. The ideal \\(\\mathfrak b\\) need not be \\(\\mathfrak m_o\\)-primary: its threshold also allows other centers containing \\(o\\), so testing only divisors centered at \\(o\\) gives an upper bound. Consequently,\n\\[\n0&lt;t\\le\\frac32,\n\\qquad\ntd_0&lt;\\frac52.\n\\]\nThe threshold is positive, finite, and rational, as is seen on a log resolution.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nc=td_0/B.\n\\]\nChoose sufficiently many general divisors \\(H_1,\\ldots,H_N\\) from the linear system spanned by the powered functions and form\n\\[\nG=\\frac cN(H_1+\\cdots+H_N).\n\\]\nChoose \\(N&gt;c\\), with generality on a log resolution of \\(\\mathfrak b\\). Write\n\\[\n\\mathfrak b\\mathcal O_Y=\\mathcal O_Y(-F),\n\\qquad\nK_Y+\\Delta_Y=\\pi_Y^*K_C.\n\\]\nAfter removing \\(F\\), the transformed system is base-point-free. Its general members \\(M_j\\) meet the existing strata with simple normal crossings, and the log pullback of \\(G\\) is\n\\[\n\\Delta_Y+cF+\\frac cN\\sum_jM_j.\n\\]</p><p>The fixed coefficients are at most one, with some equal to one at the threshold; each moving coefficient is less than one. Hence \\((C,G)\\) is lc near \\(o\\), and has an lc center. Every lc center is the image of a stratum of coefficient-one fixed components and lies in the common zero locus of the \\(f_i\\). Indeed, \\((C,0)\\) is klt, so such a fixed component has positive order on \\(\\mathfrak b\\). If the original system is one-dimensional, its moving part is empty and the same argument applies.</p><p>Each equation of \\(H_j\\) is homogeneous of weight \\(B\\). The pair is invariant, with total boundary weight \\(td_0\\). It is globally lc: a nonempty closed invariant non-lc locus would contain \\(o\\), because every grading orbit specializes to \\(o\\). The finite collection of lc centers is fixed termwise by the connected group \\(\\mathbb G_m\\), and each center contains \\(o\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The vertex \\(o\\) is not an lc center of \\((C,G)\\).", "statement": "<p>**Excluding the vertex and obtaining a minimal center, from the proof of Paper Proposition 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the setting, functions \\(f_i\\), numbers \\(d_0,t\\), and boundary \\(G\\) of Lemma 4.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\pi:\\widetilde C\\to C\\) and \\(E\\) be the extraction supplied by Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The vertex \\(o\\) is not an lc center of \\((C,G)\\).</p><p>A minimal lc center \\(Z\\) is a proper positive-dimensional homogeneous subvariety with\n\\[\n1\\le\\dim Z\\le3.\n\\]\nIt is normal and Cohen–Macaulay and admits an effective boundary making it klt.</p><p>If \\(I_Z\\subset S\\) is its homogeneous prime ideal and \\(T=S/I_Z\\), then the induced action satisfies\n\\[\n\\lambda m&lt;1\n\\]\nat every nonvertex point, even if it has a kernel, and\n\\[\nT_y=0\\qquad(0&lt;y&lt;D).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(\\widetilde G\\) for the strict transform of \\(G\\). In each smooth finite chart of the extraction, its equations depend only on the slice parameters, by homogeneity. Since the pair is lc for \\(u\\ne0\\), the slice pair is lc. Taking its product with the line and adding \\(u=0\\) preserves log canonicity, and finite descent gives\n\\[\n(\\widetilde C,E+\\widetilde G)\\quad\\text{lc}.\n\\]</p><p>The total coefficient of \\(E\\) in \\(\\pi^*G\\) is \\(td_0/\\lambda\\), so\n\\[\nK_{\\widetilde C}+E+\\widetilde G\n=\n\\pi^*(K_C+G)+(r-td_0/\\lambda)E.\n\\]\nEvery divisor \\(F'\\) centered exactly at \\(o\\) has center on \\(\\widetilde C\\) inside \\(E\\). Thus\n\\[\nA_{C,G}(F')\n=\nA_{\\widetilde C,E+\\widetilde G}(F')\n+\n(r-td_0/\\lambda)\\operatorname{ord}_{F'}(E)\n&gt;0,\n\\]\nbecause \\(td_0&lt;5/2=\\lambda r\\). This excludes the vertex as an lc center.</p><p>Choose a minimal lc center \\(Z\\). It is positive-dimensional by the preceding argument and proper because it lies in the zero locus of the nonzero functions \\(f_i\\). Therefore\n\\[\n1\\le\\dim Z\\le3.\n\\]</p><p>The affine subadjunction theorem [5, Theorem 7.2] supplies an effective boundary on \\(Z\\) making it klt. Its hypotheses hold: \\(C\\) is normal affine, \\(G\\) is an effective rational boundary, \\(K_C+G\\) is \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier, and \\((C,G)\\) is lc. In particular, \\(Z\\) is normal with rational singularities, hence Cohen–Macaulay; this implication for klt pairs does not require \\(K_Z\\) itself to be \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier [7, Theorem 120].</p><p>Let \\(I_Z\\subset S\\) be its homogeneous prime ideal and put \\(T=S/I_Z\\). Its grading is induced by the original \\(\\mathbb G_m\\)-action. The stabilizer at each nonvertex point of \\(Z\\) is the same as the stabilizer of that point in \\(C\\), so \\(\\lambda m&lt;1\\) holds even if this action on \\(Z\\) has a kernel. Each graded piece \\(T_y\\) is the image of \\(S_y\\). Every basis element \\(f_i\\) vanishes on \\(Z\\), hence\n\\[\nT_y=0\\qquad(0&lt;y&lt;D).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a homogeneous element of \\(T\\) of weight strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), and therefore of weight in \\((0,D)\\).", "statement": "<p>**The curve case, from the proof of Paper Proposition 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z\\) and \\(T=S/I_Z\\) be as in Lemma 4.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\dim Z=1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a homogeneous element of \\(T\\) of weight strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), and therefore of weight in \\((0,D)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>A normal affine curve is regular at the vertex. A homogeneous generator of its cotangent space generates its positive ideal by graded Nakayama and consequently its algebra, so\n\\[\nT=\\mathbb C[z].\n\\]\nThe integral degree of \\(z\\) is the stabilizer order at a nonzero point. Its rescaled weight is therefore \\(&lt;1&lt;D\\), by the stabilizer bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For some integer \\(q\\ge1\\), there is a homogeneous injection \\( \\omega_T^{[q]}\\longrightarrow T \\) lowering weights by a positive amount.", "statement": "<p>**The surface case, using Paper Remark 3.2 and the proof of Paper Proposition 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z\\) and \\(T=S/I_Z\\) be as in Lemma 4.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\dim Z=2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The canonical module carries the natural action on forms.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For some integer \\(q\\ge1\\), there is a homogeneous injection\n\\[\n\\omega_T^{[q]}\\longrightarrow T\n\\]\nlowering weights by a positive amount.</p>", "proof": "<p>A klt surface pair has \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factorial underlying surface; see [4, Proposition 7.2 and Corollary 7.15]. Removing the effective boundary makes \\(Z\\) itself \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Gorenstein and klt. Some \\(\\omega_T^{[q]}\\) is free at the vertex, so graded Nakayama gives a global homogeneous generator.</p><p>Its weight is positive. Indeed, divide the integral grading by its common factor to make it effective. The ineffective kernel acts identically on the variety and hence on its natural differential forms, so the intrinsic action on forms descends to that grading.</p><p>At a generic smooth base point, the grading extraction construction works for any normal \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Gorenstein positively graded cone. If a homogeneous generator of a reflexive pluricanonical power of index \\(q\\) has weight \\(w\\), its pullback has \\(u\\)-order \\(w-q\\). Thus the effective degree valuation has discrepancy \\(w/q\\). Applying this to \\(Z\\), the generator’s weight divided by \\(q\\) is positive by klt. Restoring the positive common factor preserves the sign.</p><p>Sending the generator to \\(1\\) gives the asserted injection, lowering weights by a positive amount.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a homogeneous injection \\( \\omega_T^{[q]}\\longrightarrow T \\) of degree \\(b-q(5/2)\\), hence lowering weights by the positive amount \\( q(5/2)-b>0. \\) No \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Gorenstein hypothesis on all of \\(Z\\) is …", "statement": "<p>**The threefold-center case, from the proof of Paper Proposition 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z\\) and \\(T=S/I_Z\\) be as in Lemma 4.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\dim Z=3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  V^\\circ=C\\setminus\\{o\\},\n  \\qquad\n  U=Z\\setminus\\{o\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a nonzero \\(f_i\\) from Lemma 4.1, and write\n  \\[\n  b=\\operatorname{wt}(f_i)&lt;D,\n  \\qquad\n  q=\\operatorname{ord}_Z(f_i)\\ge1.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a homogeneous injection\n\\[\n\\omega_T^{[q]}\\longrightarrow T\n\\]\nof degree \\(b-q(5/2)\\), hence lowering weights by the positive amount\n\\[\nq(5/2)-b&gt;0.\n\\]\nNo \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Gorenstein hypothesis on all of \\(Z\\) is needed.</p>", "proof": "<p>The prime divisor \\(U\\) is Cartier on smooth \\(V^\\circ\\), even where \\(U\\) itself is singular. Equivariant dualizing-sheaf adjunction gives\n\\[\n\\omega_U\n\\simeq\n\\bigl(\\omega_{V^\\circ}\\otimes\\mathcal O_{V^\\circ}(U)\\bigr)|_U.\n\\]\nThis is Cartier-divisor duality, as in [21, Section 48.14]. Applying duality to the natural Cartier sequence for \\(U\\) is compatible with the grading action, so the isomorphism carries the natural weights of differential forms.</p><p>Multiplication by \\(f_i\\) defines\n\\[\n\\mathcal O_{V^\\circ}(qU)\\longrightarrow\\mathcal O_{V^\\circ}.\n\\]\nLocally, if \\(U=(h)\\), this sends \\(h^{-q}\\) to \\(f_i/h^q\\). Its restriction to \\(U\\) is generically nonzero because the order along \\(U\\) is exactly \\(q\\).</p><p>If \\(\\eta\\) is the canonical generator of \\(C\\), divide the canonical factor in adjunction by \\(\\eta^q\\) and apply this multiplication map. We obtain\n\\[\n\\omega_U^{\\otimes q}\\longrightarrow\\mathcal O_U\n\\]\nof degree \\(b-q(5/2)\\), lowering weights by \\(q(5/2)-b&gt;0\\).</p><p>The omitted vertex has codimension three in the normal variety \\(Z\\). For \\(j:U\\hookrightarrow Z\\), reflexive extension therefore gives\n\\[\nj_*\\mathcal O_U=\\mathcal O_Z,\n\\qquad\nj_*(\\omega_U^{\\otimes q})=\\omega_Z^{[q]};\n\\]\nsee [21, Lemma 31.13.12]. Pushing forward yields a homogeneous map\n\\[\n\\omega_T^{[q]}\\longrightarrow T\n\\]\nof the same degree. It is injective because its source is torsion-free of rank one and it is generically nonzero.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "a terminal threefold section", "depth": 0, "snippet": "A general affine hyperplane section through \\(o\\) is a terminal Gorenstein threefold near \\(o\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C=\\operatorname{Spec}S\\) be the cone of Proposition 1. Thus \\(C\\) is a singular Gorenstein klt affine fourfold, smooth away from its vertex \\(o\\), with\n  \\[\n  \\widehat{\\operatorname{vol}}(o,C)\\ge162,\n  \\]\n  and with the minimizing Reeb structure supplied there.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathfrak m_o\\) be the maximal ideal of \\(o\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A normal \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Gorenstein variety is terminal when every exceptional prime divisor has log discrepancy greater than one.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A general affine hyperplane section through \\(o\\) is a terminal Gorenstein threefold near \\(o\\). In particular,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{edim}(o,C)\\le5.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a log resolution\n\\[\n\\rho:Y\\to C\n\\]\nof \\(\\mathfrak m_o\\), isomorphic away from \\(o\\), and write\n\\[\n\\mathfrak m_o\\mathcal O_Y\n=\n\\mathcal O_Y\\left(-\\sum_i b_iE_i\\right),\n\\qquad\nK_Y=\\rho^*K_C+\\sum_i(A_i-1)E_i.\n\\]\nEvery exceptional divisor has center \\(o\\), so \\(b_i\\ge1\\) is an integer and \\(A_i\\ge3\\) by Proposition 2.</p><p>For a general section \\(H\\) from the affine embedding coordinates,\n\\[\n\\rho^*H=H_Y+\\sum_i b_iE_i,\n\\]\nwhere \\(H_Y\\) is smooth and transverse to the exceptional strata.</p><p>The section \\(H\\) is normal Gorenstein near \\(o\\): it is a Cartier section of a Gorenstein Cohen–Macaulay fourfold, and is smooth away from \\(o\\) by Bertini. Thus it is \\(S_2\\) and \\(R_1\\), and is generically reduced, giving normality.</p><p>Proposition 4 yields\n\\[\nA_i/b_i&gt;\\frac32.\n\\]\nIf \\(b_i\\ge2\\), then\n\\[\nA_i-b_i&gt;\\frac{b_i}{2}\\ge1.\n\\]\nIf \\(b_i=1\\), the bound \\(A_i\\ge3\\) gives\n\\[\nA_i-b_i\\ge2.\n\\]\nHence every coefficient \\(A_i-1-b_i\\) is strictly positive.</p><p>Adjunction on \\(H_Y\\) gives precisely these ordinary discrepancy coefficients along the components of \\(E_i|_{H_Y}\\). Subsequent exceptional divisors over the smooth \\(H_Y\\) have positive ordinary discrepancy, and the pullback of the already effective relative canonical divisor cannot decrease it. Thus \\(H\\) is terminal.</p><p>A singular Gorenstein terminal threefold point is an isolated compound Du Val point by [18, Theorem (1.1)], hence an analytic hypersurface by [18, Section (0.5)]. A smooth point also has embedding dimension at most four. Therefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{edim}(o,H)\\le4.\n\\]\nQuotienting by one local equation gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{edim}(o,C)\n\\le\n\\operatorname{edim}(o,H)+1\n\\le5.\n\\]</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 3, "cite"], [1, 6, "cite"], [1, 10, "cite"], [1, 16, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [3, 6, "cite"], [3, 10, "cite"], [3, 12, "cite"], [3, 16, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [6, 10, "cite"], [6, 11, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [7, 9, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [9, 6, "cite"], [10, 16, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [11, 15, "cite"], [12, 10, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [12, 14, "cite"], [12, 15, "cite"], [13, 10, "cite"], [14, 10, "cite"], [15, 10, "cite"], [16, 0, "cite"]]});
